 ### Global gotchas (cross-cutting — module-specific ones live in the PRD sections)
 
 - **`mailbox: 'careers'` on `queueEmail` is load-bearing.** The global SMTP is deliberately minimal in prod — a candidate email queued without it silently no-ops. Any candidate-facing email must carry it; staff/system mail uses the default mailbox (Gmail-as-info@ fallback).
 - **Never render a user-facing time with bare `toLocaleString()`** — containers run UTC. Use `formatWhenForCountry()` / `formatWhen()` from `common/utils/datetime.util.ts` (and `hourCycle:'h12'`, never `hour12:true`).
 - **PDFKit's built-in fonts are WinAnsi and silently garble Turkish** — any text run in a generated PDF must use the registered `PDF_FONT` Noto aliases; resolve bundled assets via `common/utils/assets.util.ts`, never hand-counted `../`.
-- **No public endpoints outside `careers-public`** (auth is the one other sanctioned public surface). Don't sprinkle `@Public()` on feature modules.
+- **No public endpoints outside `careers-public`, auth, and the argued machine doors** — the token-guarded agent routes (İŞKUR/Indeed), the signed webhooks (PRD §7.8, §7.9) and the website intake door `website/v1` (`@Public()` + `WebsiteModuleEnabledGuard` + `WebsiteApiGuard`, PRD §7.10). Each of those was sanctioned BY NAME in the PRD in the same change set that added it; a new one needs the same written argument, its own module, and the careers-public hardening set (fail-closed token, captcha, IP hashing, dedupe, audit visibility). Don't sprinkle `@Public()` on feature modules.
 - **The AI-interview recording pipeline is ADD-ONLY** — never introduce a delete of a stored recording object; the only sanctioned deleter is the retention sweep (dry-run unless `AI_RETENTION_PURGE_LIVE=true`).
 - **`PERMISSION_SCOPE_ENFORCEMENT` is OFF in prod** (not to be confused with `PERMISSIONS_STRICT`, which is ON and gates nothing — it labels the conformance report; PRD §4.8) — scoped roles are protected only by `resolveEnforced()` call sites in **projects, sales + whatsapp**. Don't revert those to `resolve()`; a new scoped read path needs `resolveEnforced()` too. **Pass the key the ROUTE is gated on** (`sales.leads`, not `sales`) — the grant resolves at that key and falls back to the module row, and `repo-invariants.spec.ts` lists every call site with its key (PRD §4.8). **The flag is not the control that matters** (assessed 2026-08-10): it gates one method with zero callers and one used only where every grant is already scope `ALL`, so enabling it is a verified no-op. Fix call sites, not the flag.
 - **Never assign to `where.OR` in a list query that spreads a scope fragment — append to `where.AND`.** The `ASSIGNED_TO_ME` bindings are `OR` **arrays**, so `where.OR = [...search terms]` overwrote the row-level restriction outright and a scoped user saw the entire table by typing one character into the search box. Found live in sales, HRM (passport/ID/bank) and IR on 2026-08-10. The city filter in the same function was already guarded against this collision — the scope fragment was the case nobody thought of.
 - **A required placeholder/field must be able to arrive EMPTY.** Any gate stated over *rendered output* (the offer/contract completeness gate counts `[—]` glyphs) is structurally blind to a required value whose resolution chain ends in a non-empty literal — it prints a made-up answer instead of refusing. A blank salary rendered the advertised band floor (`PKR 60.00`), a blank job title rendered `'the role'`. The **type** is usually the root cause: `title: string` forces a fallback at the call site; `title?: string` lets the gate work.
 - **Persist what a document PRINTED, not the form fields it was sent.** A value that falls back to a mutable settings row and is stored as `null` exists nowhere, so the next render re-derives it and two "identical" documents can silently disagree. Snapshot the resolved value.
 - **Don't hard-code role slugs for authorization** — derive capability from a grant (`@RequirePermission`, `EffectivePermissionsService.check/checkAny`, `permissions.can(...)`, `can()` in the browser). `repo-invariants.spec.ts` fails on a new `roleSlug === 'super-admin'` / `{ slug: 'super-admin' }` outside its named allowlist. Every literal W4 removed was a capability the model could already express, written as an identity — so it had no cell on the Roles screen, could not be delegated, could not be revoked for one person, and was invisible to anyone auditing what a role can do (PRD §4.1).
